1
00:00:03,460 --> 00:00:05,290
- Morning.     - Hi.

2
00:00:05,750 --> 00:00:07,290
How was your date?

3
00:00:07,840 --> 00:00:09,880
- Pretty good.     - Oh, good.

4
00:00:12,220 --> 00:00:14,380
- Aah!     - My God. Sorry about that.

5
00:00:15,890 --> 00:00:17,850
Okay, really good.

6
00:00:18,100 --> 00:00:19,640
I gotta go. I'm late.

7
00:00:19,850 --> 00:00:21,930
You're going to leave her with me?

8
00:00:22,140 --> 00:00:24,180
Don't worry, she's a terrific girl.

9
00:00:24,440 --> 00:00:25,770
Can you do me a favor?

10
00:00:26,020 --> 00:00:30,400
Could you mention I'm not looking for a relationship? That'd be great.

11
00:00:30,730 --> 00:00:32,570
What, are you kidding?

12
00:00:32,780 --> 00:00:36,200
Just casually slip it in, you know. Lay the groundwork.

13
00:00:36,450 --> 00:00:39,700
Tell her I'm a loner. No, an outlaw.

14
00:00:39,950 --> 00:00:43,040
She doesn't want to get mixed up with the likes of me.

15
00:00:43,250 --> 00:00:44,620
That's a lot to remember.

16
00:00:44,870 --> 00:00:46,960
Can't I tell her that you're a pig?

17
00:00:47,710 --> 00:00:49,960
I'm gonna call her later myself.

18
00:00:50,210 --> 00:00:51,420
Honest.

19
00:00:51,670 --> 00:00:54,720
Chandler used to do it. He'd make her pancakes.

20
00:00:54,970 --> 00:00:57,630
He'd make extras and leave them for me.

21
00:00:58,140 --> 00:01:01,390
I'm not telling her anything. It's not my responsibility.

22
00:01:01,600 --> 00:01:02,850
Fine.

23
00:01:04,140 --> 00:01:06,520
Now, where did we land on those pancakes?

24
00:01:10,440 --> 00:01:11,610
- Hi.     - Hi.

25
00:01:11,820 --> 00:01:15,030
Sorry, but I couldn't get that lock to work.

26
00:01:15,240 --> 00:01:18,320
Yeah, Joey kind of disabled it when I moved in.

27
00:01:18,990 --> 00:01:21,700
- You must be Rachel. I'm Erin.     - Hi.

28
00:01:22,410 --> 00:01:26,910
I don't mean this to sound like high school, but did he talk about me?

29
00:01:29,710 --> 00:01:31,250
Would you like some pancakes?

30
00:02:09,580 --> 00:02:11,450

31
00:02:13,380 --> 00:02:17,260
- Come on. Why are we here?     - Okay, okay, take a guess.

32
00:02:18,760 --> 00:02:20,220
The hot chicks?

33
00:02:20,890 --> 00:02:24,890
Okay, okay, I was typing names into the library computer earlier...

34
00:02:25,140 --> 00:02:26,310
...you know, for fun.

35
00:02:27,020 --> 00:02:29,770
And I typed mine in, and guess what came up?

36
00:02:30,020 --> 00:02:31,850
My doctoral dissertation.

37
00:02:32,110 --> 00:02:36,150
It's right down here in the biggest library in the university.

38
00:02:36,400 --> 00:02:39,030
Wow, that's actually pretty cool.

39
00:02:39,650 --> 00:02:44,120
There's also a book here by a woman named Wendy Bagina.

40
00:02:47,040 --> 00:02:48,330
What is that?

41
00:02:49,330 --> 00:02:53,420
Sounds like two people are really enjoying the Dewey decimal system.

42
00:02:57,880 --> 00:03:00,220
We're so sorry.

43
00:03:03,640 --> 00:03:06,260
You didn't bring me here to do that, did you?

44
00:03:12,900 --> 00:03:14,650
She sent the chicken back again?

45
00:03:14,860 --> 00:03:18,980
She said it's too dry and wants to explain to you how she wants it.

46
00:03:19,240 --> 00:03:21,530
I'd like to meet this chicken expert.

47
00:03:21,780 --> 00:03:23,530
Send the colonel in.

48
00:03:28,160 --> 00:03:32,330
Oh, my God!

49
00:03:34,290 --> 00:03:35,880
Lucky bastard.

50
00:03:37,550 --> 00:03:39,460
- Janice.     - How are you...

51
00:03:39,670 --> 00:03:44,800
...Ms. Hotshot-Chef-at-the-Big-Fancy- Restaurant-With-the-Best-Chicken-Ever?

52
00:03:47,640 --> 00:03:49,220
I'm fine.

53
00:03:49,430 --> 00:03:52,890
Oh, what is that on your finger? I'm blind.

54
00:03:53,560 --> 00:03:55,350
Oh, uh...

55
00:03:55,560 --> 00:03:58,270
So who's the lucky guy?

56
00:04:00,990 --> 00:04:03,530
Oh, my God.

57
00:04:04,740 --> 00:04:07,120
I am so sorry, sweetie.

58
00:04:07,330 --> 00:04:09,240
Are you okay?

59
00:04:10,370 --> 00:04:12,200
You didn't tell her we're engaged?

60
00:04:12,410 --> 00:04:14,920
- She saw the ring.     - Did she freak out?

61
00:04:15,120 --> 00:04:18,380
She was shocked, but then again, so were most people.

62
00:04:18,540 --> 00:04:19,960
Eh.

63
00:04:20,340 --> 00:04:22,970
She actually has a boyfriend herself.

64
00:04:23,380 --> 00:04:24,880
Name's Clark.

65
00:04:25,130 --> 00:04:27,930
She also invited herself to our wedding.

66
00:04:28,100 --> 00:04:29,810
Clark too.

67
00:04:31,640 --> 00:04:32,930
You said no, right?

68
00:04:34,140 --> 00:04:35,390
Huh?

69
00:04:37,020 --> 00:04:39,110
You said no, right?

70
00:04:39,320 --> 00:04:42,440
She cornered me. She asked me if the wedding was in town.

71
00:04:42,690 --> 00:04:44,610
- What was I supposed to do?     - Lie.

72
00:04:46,030 --> 00:04:48,370
How hard is that? "Your check's in the mail."

73
00:04:48,620 --> 00:04:51,660
"Oh, your baby's so cute."

74
00:04:51,830 --> 00:04:55,540
"I can't wait to read your book, Ross."

75
00:04:57,250 --> 00:05:00,090
So she comes to the wedding. Maybe it won't be so bad.

76
00:05:00,300 --> 00:05:04,130
Think she'll sit quietly? Think she'll want to make a toast?

77
00:05:04,380 --> 00:05:08,140
You don't think she'll want to sing "Part-Time Lover"?

78
00:05:08,890 --> 00:05:12,810
Oh, my God. She's not gonna like the chicken either, is she?

79
00:05:15,810 --> 00:05:17,810
You know what? It'll be okay.

80
00:05:17,980 --> 00:05:20,560
She's probably not gonna even want to come.

81
00:05:20,730 --> 00:05:24,110
- Really?     - No, that was a lie.

82
00:05:24,610 --> 00:05:26,650
See how easy that was?

83
00:05:27,530 --> 00:05:30,410
So you would have just lied?

84
00:05:30,660 --> 00:05:32,030
Yes.

85
00:05:32,330 --> 00:05:36,200
- It would have really been that easy?     - Yes.

86
00:05:36,410 --> 00:05:39,500
Do it Saturday. We're having dinner with her and Clark.

87
00:05:46,880 --> 00:05:47,920
Hey, what's up?

88
00:05:48,130 --> 00:05:49,550
- Hey.     - Hey.

89
00:05:50,180 --> 00:05:52,510
Hey, who's your friend?

90
00:05:52,760 --> 00:05:54,140
Hey!

91
00:05:54,850 --> 00:05:56,680
- Hey, Joey.     - Erin.

92
00:05:57,770 --> 00:05:59,060
You're still here.

93
00:06:00,650 --> 00:06:04,690
We ended up spending the day together and had such a great time.

94
00:06:04,900 --> 00:06:07,690
Why wouldn't you? Erin is great.

95
00:06:07,950 --> 00:06:10,240
And then there's you guys.

96
00:06:10,490 --> 00:06:13,780
Well, listen, I better get going.

97
00:06:13,950 --> 00:06:15,450
- Today was great. Thanks.     - I know.

98
00:06:15,660 --> 00:06:16,700
Okay.

99
00:06:16,910 --> 00:06:19,370
And Joey, last night was fun.

100
00:06:19,580 --> 00:06:21,960
Yeah. I'll call you.

101
00:06:22,170 --> 00:06:24,090
- I'll call you too.     - Or I'll call you.

102
00:06:24,340 --> 00:06:25,960
- And call me.     - Okay.

103
00:06:26,760 --> 00:06:28,170
- Good to see you again.     - Bye.

104
00:06:28,420 --> 00:06:30,470
- Bye-bye.     - Bye.

105
00:06:31,970 --> 00:06:35,010
The system kind of broke down, huh?

106
00:06:35,930 --> 00:06:39,520
Joey, I'm sorry. I just couldn't tell her all those things.

107
00:06:39,730 --> 00:06:41,520
And we got to talking, and I...

108
00:06:41,770 --> 00:06:43,730
We want you to marry her.

109
00:06:44,690 --> 00:06:45,730
What?

110
00:06:45,940 --> 00:06:48,440
She is so amazing. You have no idea.

111
00:06:48,650 --> 00:06:50,530
Who do you think brought her here?

112
00:06:50,780 --> 00:06:52,110
Cupid.

113
00:06:54,330 --> 00:06:56,790
She's so cool. She speaks four languages.

114
00:06:56,990 --> 00:07:00,160
Man, do you know what guys want.

115
00:07:03,130 --> 00:07:06,500
Come on, she's so perfect for you. She's sweet.

116
00:07:06,750 --> 00:07:10,050
She likes baseball. She had two beers at lunch.

117
00:07:10,260 --> 00:07:11,800
My beers?!

118
00:07:13,430 --> 00:07:15,260
Look, she's a very nice girl.

119
00:07:15,470 --> 00:07:18,520
We had a very good time, but I don't see it going anywhere.

120
00:07:18,720 --> 00:07:20,140
You always say that.

121
00:07:20,350 --> 00:07:23,230
Maybe if you gave her a chance, it would go somewhere.

122
00:07:23,440 --> 00:07:26,230
I'm sorry, you guys. I just don't think so.

123
00:07:26,900 --> 00:07:28,690
- Whatever.     - Fine.

124
00:07:28,900 --> 00:07:31,740
Hey, don't start judging me.

125
00:07:32,030 --> 00:07:33,910
You're in love with your assistant.

126
00:07:34,530 --> 00:07:37,870
You're sleeping with the guy who keeps pigeons on the roof.

127
00:07:38,870 --> 00:07:40,080
Phoebe!

128
00:07:40,370 --> 00:07:42,370
Secret affair.

129
00:07:46,670 --> 00:07:48,330

130
00:07:52,220 --> 00:07:55,090
People are doing it in front of my book.

131
00:07:56,760 --> 00:07:57,970
I'm sorry?

132
00:07:58,140 --> 00:08:02,480
My doctoral dissertation is in the library at school. I went to see it.

133
00:08:02,640 --> 00:08:08,230
And there were students making babies right in the paleontology section.

134
00:08:08,650 --> 00:08:11,860
Oh, my God. Did you get to see anything good?

135
00:08:13,030 --> 00:08:14,280
At your school...

136
00:08:14,490 --> 00:08:19,620
...was there a place on campus where students went to fool around?

137
00:08:19,950 --> 00:08:21,080
Yeah, there was.

138
00:08:21,330 --> 00:08:25,790
In the corner of the library where all the books were that nobody ever read.

139
00:08:26,040 --> 00:08:28,130
Yes, there was.

140
00:08:28,500 --> 00:08:32,510
Great, because people kept showing up. I think it's like a thing.

141
00:08:32,880 --> 00:08:34,420
Hold on a second.

142
00:08:34,680 --> 00:08:37,930
Fifth floor, against that back wall?

143
00:08:39,810 --> 00:08:41,680
Oh, for crying out loud!

144
00:08:44,940 --> 00:08:47,440
- So we should go catch our movie.     - What's the rush?

145
00:08:47,690 --> 00:08:50,360
I'd like to see the previews.

146
00:08:51,280 --> 00:08:52,860
The candy.

147
00:08:54,200 --> 00:08:57,570
- Oh, hey.     - Well, look who's here.

148
00:08:57,820 --> 00:08:59,320
- Hey, Joey.     - Hey, Erin.

149
00:08:59,530 --> 00:09:00,780
- Hey, Rachel.      - Hi.

150
00:09:00,990 --> 00:09:03,620
We were about to take off and see a movie.

151
00:09:04,210 --> 00:09:07,420
- Oh, no!     - What's wrong?

152
00:09:07,630 --> 00:09:11,840
Phoebe, we forgot that party we have to go to.

153
00:09:12,050 --> 00:09:13,920
Oh, no.

154
00:09:14,920 --> 00:09:15,970
What party?

155
00:09:16,260 --> 00:09:18,090
A birthday party.

156
00:09:19,220 --> 00:09:20,640
Whose birthday party?

157
00:09:20,890 --> 00:09:23,100
Alison's birthday party.

158
00:09:24,600 --> 00:09:26,940
Oh. And how old is Alison?

159
00:09:27,190 --> 00:09:28,560
Thirty-two.

160
00:09:31,440 --> 00:09:35,400
Wait a minute. Why don't you guys do something?

161
00:09:35,610 --> 00:09:38,280
Look how that worked out.

162
00:09:43,580 --> 00:09:46,500
Excuse me. Hi, I'm a professor here.

163
00:09:46,750 --> 00:09:51,540
Do you know the paleontology section, fifth floor, stack 437?

164
00:09:51,750 --> 00:09:54,630
Well, yes. Just give me five minutes.

165
00:09:54,880 --> 00:09:58,130
I just have to find someone to cover my shift.

166
00:10:00,430 --> 00:10:03,390
No, no, no.

167
00:10:03,640 --> 00:10:05,640
Can I speak to someone in charge?

168
00:10:09,560 --> 00:10:10,560
How can I help?

169
00:10:10,730 --> 00:10:14,110
Hi, I was wondering, is it possible to increase security...

170
00:10:14,360 --> 00:10:16,280
...in the paleontology section?

171
00:10:16,490 --> 00:10:18,190
I wrote a book that's up there.

172
00:10:18,450 --> 00:10:20,780
And instead of reading it, people are...

173
00:10:21,030 --> 00:10:24,160
Well, they're rolling around in front of it.

174
00:10:25,240 --> 00:10:29,080
We are aware of the problem you're referring to.

175
00:10:33,340 --> 00:10:37,050
As far as increasing security, I'm afraid the library's understaffed.

176
00:10:37,300 --> 00:10:39,720
I can't help you.

177
00:10:40,220 --> 00:10:41,760
Well, fine.

178
00:10:41,930 --> 00:10:44,680
If I'm the only person with any appreciation...

179
00:10:44,850 --> 00:10:48,680
...of the sanctity of the written word, then I'll go defend it myself.

180
00:10:49,310 --> 00:10:51,690
Don't you follow me.

181
00:10:54,320 --> 00:10:55,770
- Hey.    - Hey.

182
00:10:56,150 --> 00:10:59,150
- How did it go with Erin?     - Unbelievable.

183
00:10:59,400 --> 00:11:01,360
We had the best time.

184
00:11:01,530 --> 00:11:02,530
Yay!

185
00:11:02,700 --> 00:11:06,160
- So you're not mad anymore?     - No, no. You guys were totally right.

186
00:11:06,370 --> 00:11:08,620
This was much better than the first date.

187
00:11:08,830 --> 00:11:11,370
It was awkward. We were both nervous.

188
00:11:11,620 --> 00:11:14,670
- Didn't you sleep together?     - Yeah, that calms me down.

189
00:11:16,800 --> 00:11:18,670
And we have so much in common.

190
00:11:18,920 --> 00:11:24,340
She loves sandwiches, sports. Although she is a Met fan.

191
00:11:24,510 --> 00:11:27,060
Not an issue now, but if we had kids...

192
00:11:27,220 --> 00:11:31,350
Oh, my God! Listen to you talking about having kids.

193
00:11:32,140 --> 00:11:34,900
Oh, my Joey.

194
00:11:35,150 --> 00:11:37,860
- Please don't get married before I do.     - Oh.

195
00:11:41,740 --> 00:11:43,490
Oh!

196
00:11:43,700 --> 00:11:47,370
I just cannot believe that Clark stood me up.

197
00:11:47,830 --> 00:11:49,080
He may still show up.

198
00:11:49,290 --> 00:11:50,540
What are you, stupid?

199
00:11:51,870 --> 00:11:55,330
- It's been three hours.     - Is that all?

200
00:11:56,290 --> 00:11:59,920
Let's go on to happier things.

201
00:12:00,130 --> 00:12:02,670
Why don't you tell me about your lovely wedding?

202
00:12:02,880 --> 00:12:07,680
There was something that we wanted to tell you about the wedding.

203
00:12:07,930 --> 00:12:11,180
It's going to be a small ceremony.

204
00:12:11,430 --> 00:12:13,310
Tiny.

205
00:12:14,150 --> 00:12:17,150
We're not even sure why we're having it.

206
00:12:17,730 --> 00:12:20,900
It's actually going to be just family.

207
00:12:22,110 --> 00:12:25,570
Oh, wait.

208
00:12:26,870 --> 00:12:29,450
You two think of me as family?

209
00:12:33,160 --> 00:12:36,420
I have to ask you something now, and be honest.

210
00:12:36,630 --> 00:12:41,250
Do you want me to sing "Careless Whisper" or "Lady in Red"?

211
00:12:44,760 --> 00:12:46,890
How can you say that? The Mets have no closer.

212
00:12:47,140 --> 00:12:50,390
- What about Benitez?     - What about game one of the Series?

213
00:12:50,640 --> 00:12:53,100
- What about shut up?     - You shut up.

214
00:12:53,930 --> 00:12:56,060
I love arguing with her.

215
00:12:56,310 --> 00:12:58,400
- Be right back.     - Okay.

216
00:12:59,860 --> 00:13:01,150
How's it going with Joey?

217
00:13:01,360 --> 00:13:03,320
Oh, okay.

218
00:13:03,570 --> 00:13:04,940
Okay?

219
00:13:05,150 --> 00:13:09,160
Tell me that you like him, please. I mean, tell me that you like him.

220
00:13:09,580 --> 00:13:13,750
He's a really great guy, and I know you really want this to work out...

221
00:13:13,950 --> 00:13:16,920
...but I just don't see this having a future.

222
00:13:17,170 --> 00:13:18,420
What?

223
00:13:19,460 --> 00:13:22,340
But you said that you liked him. What happened?

224
00:13:22,590 --> 00:13:25,340
Did you just change your mind?

225
00:13:25,840 --> 00:13:28,550
- Kind of.     - Then change it back!

226
00:13:29,680 --> 00:13:33,390
I'm sorry. It's just there's no real spark.

227
00:13:33,600 --> 00:13:35,430
Didn't you sleep together?

228
00:13:35,850 --> 00:13:37,060
Yeah.

229
00:13:37,310 --> 00:13:38,980
Tramp.

230
00:13:40,820 --> 00:13:42,360
Does Joey have any idea?

231
00:13:42,610 --> 00:13:44,940
I don't think he does. You know what?

232
00:13:45,190 --> 00:13:47,950
Maybe you could tell him I'm not interested...

233
00:13:48,200 --> 00:13:50,110
...in a serious relationship.

234
00:13:51,450 --> 00:13:53,790
You mean, like, that you're kind of a loner?

235
00:13:53,990 --> 00:13:55,410
Yeah, that'd be great.

236
00:13:55,750 --> 00:13:59,500
- And maybe that you're a real stronzo.     - I'm sorry?

237
00:13:59,670 --> 00:14:03,380
I guess Italian isn't one of the four languages you speak.

238
00:14:05,760 --> 00:14:08,380
- Hey. Want to go?     - Yeah, let's go.

239
00:14:08,630 --> 00:14:11,890
- Okay. See you guys later.     - Bye, guys.

240
00:14:12,600 --> 00:14:14,810
Hey, thank you so much.

241
00:14:18,390 --> 00:14:19,520
Wow.

242
00:14:19,770 --> 00:14:22,560
I guess it wasn't Cupid that brought her here.

243
00:14:22,820 --> 00:14:26,360
No, just a regular old flying dwarf.

244
00:14:33,280 --> 00:14:36,040
Yes, yes. How can I help you?

245
00:14:36,200 --> 00:14:38,000
We were just looking around.

246
00:14:38,210 --> 00:14:40,580
Oh, you're fellow scholars.

247
00:14:40,750 --> 00:14:43,250
What exactly were you looking for?

248
00:14:43,420 --> 00:14:44,420
Perhaps...

249
00:14:44,590 --> 00:14:47,590
Perhaps Dr. Chester Stock's musings...

250
00:14:47,800 --> 00:14:50,590
...on the Smilodon californicus?

251
00:14:51,930 --> 00:14:53,090
- Uh...     - Uh! Uh!

252
00:14:53,300 --> 00:14:54,430
Get out of here!

253
00:15:04,110 --> 00:15:05,650
Meeting someone?

254
00:15:05,860 --> 00:15:08,570
Or are you just here to brush up...

255
00:15:08,820 --> 00:15:11,740
...on Merriam's views on evolution?

256
00:15:12,030 --> 00:15:16,620
Actually, I find Merriam's views far too progressionist.

257
00:15:21,250 --> 00:15:24,540
I find Merriam's views far too progressionist.

258
00:15:24,790 --> 00:15:27,960
I'm sorry. Who are you?

259
00:15:28,170 --> 00:15:30,380
I'm a professor here. Ross Geller.

260
00:15:30,630 --> 00:15:34,300
Ross Geller? Why do I know that name? It's a...

261
00:15:34,680 --> 00:15:35,720
Wait.

262
00:15:39,730 --> 00:15:41,020
Did you write this?

263
00:15:41,890 --> 00:15:43,390
Yes.

264
00:15:46,570 --> 00:15:50,490
You're the person who checked out my book?

265
00:15:51,610 --> 00:15:56,410
You know, you look nothing like I would have thought.

266
00:15:56,830 --> 00:15:58,330
You're so young.

267
00:15:59,500 --> 00:16:03,540
Well, I skipped fourth grade.

268
00:16:11,170 --> 00:16:15,640
I am very, very sorry.

269
00:16:21,730 --> 00:16:22,890
- Hey.     - Hey.

270
00:16:23,140 --> 00:16:24,140
Hi.

271
00:16:24,310 --> 00:16:28,230
- So how was your date?     - Great. We walked around the Village.

272
00:16:28,400 --> 00:16:31,030
We went to this ice cream place. Split a milk shake.

273
00:16:31,280 --> 00:16:32,940
Seventy-thirty, but still.

274
00:16:34,030 --> 00:16:37,780
I'm thinking of taking her upstate to one of those bed-and-breakfasts.

275
00:16:37,990 --> 00:16:40,280
Really? She said she wants to go?

276
00:16:40,540 --> 00:16:45,160
No, it's a surprise. It's gonna be tricky, because she's busy at work.

277
00:16:45,420 --> 00:16:46,670
- Oh.     - Oh.

278
00:16:47,170 --> 00:16:50,500
Joey, look, honey, we need to talk, okay?

279
00:16:51,130 --> 00:16:52,710
Um...

280
00:16:52,920 --> 00:16:54,920
I got the feeling from her...

281
00:16:55,180 --> 00:16:59,600
...that she's not looking for a serious relationship.

282
00:17:00,720 --> 00:17:03,310
- Where are you getting this?     - She told me.

283
00:17:05,270 --> 00:17:07,520
She said she's kind of a loner.

284
00:17:08,770 --> 00:17:14,190
Oh.

285
00:17:16,070 --> 00:17:18,360
- Joey...     - Hey, Rach, it's cool, you know.

286
00:17:18,570 --> 00:17:21,280
I'm a loner too, right?

287
00:17:22,410 --> 00:17:25,750
Hey, Joey. You know what? You are way too good for her.

288
00:17:25,960 --> 00:17:30,880
I promise, next time I will tell them you're not looking for a relationship.

289
00:17:31,300 --> 00:17:32,550
No.

290
00:17:33,550 --> 00:17:36,720
No, don't do that. Just...

291
00:17:38,590 --> 00:17:41,720
- Next time, make sure she likes me.     - Well, that too.

292
00:17:46,890 --> 00:17:48,190
Joey?

293
00:17:49,520 --> 00:17:50,690
Yeah?

294
00:17:51,520 --> 00:17:53,610
- Do you want some pancakes?     - Finally.

295
00:17:57,610 --> 00:17:59,200
What are we gonna do?

296
00:17:59,700 --> 00:18:01,450
I say we go with "Careless Whisper."

297
00:18:05,500 --> 00:18:06,620
Chandler?

298
00:18:07,000 --> 00:18:09,460
Did she see us yet? Did she see us?

299
00:18:14,340 --> 00:18:16,340
Janice, what are you doing here?

300
00:18:16,590 --> 00:18:19,380
I thought I was gonna go back to my apartment...

301
00:18:19,640 --> 00:18:24,850
...but I just felt like I really couldn't be alone tonight.

302
00:18:30,350 --> 00:18:34,110
I was just wondering if I could maybe stay here with you.

303
00:18:34,360 --> 00:18:37,690
Just I really feel like I need to be with family.

304
00:18:39,700 --> 00:18:42,910
Our kids are gonna call her Aunt Janice, aren't they?

305
00:18:43,410 --> 00:18:48,330
Please. Because otherwise, I really don't know what I might do.

306
00:18:48,870 --> 00:18:52,630
Aren't you just a teensy bit curious?

307
00:18:54,040 --> 00:18:56,630
- You have any tissues?     - Yeah, in the bathroom.

308
00:19:00,220 --> 00:19:01,970
We'll just let her stay.

309
00:19:02,180 --> 00:19:06,600
No. If we let her stay, she will stay forever.

310
00:19:07,600 --> 00:19:10,850
- Kind of like your Barcalounger.     - Is that what you're thinking about?

311
00:19:11,060 --> 00:19:12,850
I never stop thinking about it.

312
00:19:14,730 --> 00:19:16,070
Hey, you guys?

313
00:19:16,280 --> 00:19:19,320
Do either one of you want to get in there before I take my bath?

314
00:19:21,700 --> 00:19:23,530
Janice, I'm sorry...

315
00:19:23,780 --> 00:19:27,120
...but you can't stay here tonight.

316
00:19:27,740 --> 00:19:28,790
Why not?

317
00:19:29,000 --> 00:19:30,580
Honestly?

318
00:19:30,830 --> 00:19:34,040
Our apartment is a hotbed...

319
00:19:34,290 --> 00:19:36,840
...for electromagnetic activity.

320
00:19:38,670 --> 00:19:41,670
Monica and I have been immunized, but sadly...

321
00:19:41,930 --> 00:19:45,470
...you have not.

322
00:19:51,230 --> 00:19:52,770
I'm gonna need a comforter.

323
00:19:53,020 --> 00:19:56,520
Do you have a hypoallergenic one? Because otherwise I get very nasal.

324
00:20:00,440 --> 00:20:03,360
Do you have a cat? Because it's already happening.

325
00:20:03,530 --> 00:20:04,780
Eh. Do you hear that? Listen.

326
00:20:09,870 --> 00:20:12,250
Oh, my God! Oh, my God! You have to go.

327
00:20:12,460 --> 00:20:13,460
Why?

328
00:20:16,210 --> 00:20:18,790
Because Chandler still has feelings for you.

329
00:20:22,380 --> 00:20:23,550
He does?

330
00:20:23,970 --> 00:20:25,640
Say again?

331
00:20:26,350 --> 00:20:28,680
That's right. That's right.

332
00:20:28,890 --> 00:20:31,970
And that is why you can't stay here tonight.

333
00:20:32,230 --> 00:20:35,350
And probably why you shouldn't come to the wedding.

334
00:20:36,230 --> 00:20:39,440
Feelings. Such strong feelings.

335
00:20:40,820 --> 00:20:45,660
I mean, I realize that his feelings may never completely go away...

336
00:20:45,860 --> 00:20:47,910
...but you can.

337
00:20:49,030 --> 00:20:51,830
Oh, my God.

338
00:20:52,080 --> 00:20:55,710
I understand. I am so sorry.

339
00:20:55,920 --> 00:20:57,420
I'll go.

340
00:21:02,510 --> 00:21:04,220
Goodbye, Monica.

341
00:21:04,470 --> 00:21:08,510
I wish you a lifetime of happiness with him.

342
00:21:08,720 --> 00:21:09,760
Chandler.

343
00:21:11,770 --> 00:21:14,060
You call me when this goes in the pooper.

344
00:21:27,280 --> 00:21:29,280
I've got this section covered.

345
00:21:29,450 --> 00:21:32,490
Yeah, in fact, I've got this baby to shine in people's eyes.

346
00:21:32,660 --> 00:21:34,370
Okay, see you later.

347
00:21:44,630 --> 00:21:46,970
I just wanted to show Monica your book.

